get.description:
Whether you have declared professional or non-professional use of market data.

`attested: true` means a `non_professional` declaration is on record, which some data requires. `classification` and `submitted_at` appear once you have declared. `locked: true` means a `professional` declaration is on record, which cannot be changed through the API.

#### Example

```http
GET /v2/account/classification
```

```json
{
  "data": {
    "attested": true,
    "submitted_at": 1789185753185,
    "classification": "non_professional",
    "locked": false
  },
  "meta": {
    "caps": {
      "tier": "pro",
      "flow": true,
      "applied": []
    }
  }
}
```

post.description:
Records whether you use market data as a professional or non-professional subscriber, and returns the stored record.

The body is `{"classification": "non_professional"}` or `{"classification": "professional"}`. A `non_professional` declaration unlocks the data that requires it from your next request. A `professional` declaration unlocks nothing and is final: a later `non_professional` submit returns the existing record with `locked: true`.

#### Example

```http
POST /v2/account/classification
```

```json
{
  "data": {
    "attested": true,
    "submitted_at": 1789185753185,
    "classification": "non_professional",
    "locked": false
  },
  "meta": {
    "caps": {
      "tier": "pro",
      "flow": true,
      "applied": []
    }
  }
}
```

get.description:
Your request counts per day over the last 30 days, by scope group, with totals and your current rate limit.

Scope groups are this document's tags, such as `analytics.gex`. `rate_limit.per_min` matches the `x-ratelimit-limit` header, and `quota` is not reported yet.

#### Units

`date` is a UTC calendar day.

#### Freshness

Counts are provisional, not billing figures, and may miss some requests.

#### Empty results

Before your first request, `by_day` is empty and `totals_by_scope_group` is `{}`.

#### Example

One day and scope group:

```http
GET /v2/account/usage
```

```json
{
  "data": {
    "trailing_days": 30,
    "by_day": [
      {
        "date": "2026-09-27",
        "scope_group": "analytics.gex",
        "count": 4
      }
    ],
    "totals_by_scope_group": {
      "analytics.gex": 4
    },
    "rate_limit": {
      "per_min": 600
    }
  },
  "meta": {
    "caps": {
      "tier": "pro",
      "applied": []
    }
  }
}
```

One underlying's cumulative call and put volume, premium and trade counts for a session, by strike and by expiry.

`coverage.status` is `observed_live`, with `coverage.complete: false`, until the end-of-day reconciliation replaces the section with `eod_reconciled` coverage. Needs a Pro app session or an API Pro key, plus a non-professional declaration.

#### Units

Volumes are contracts, and `*_trade_count` counts prints.

#### Freshness

Updated as the session trades. Sessions from about the last 24 hours are held.

#### Empty results

`snapshot` is `null`, with a `meta.note`, when no section has been observed for the symbol and session.

#### Example

One strike and one expiry (`by_strike`, `by_expiration` and `meta` truncated):

```http
GET /v2/flow/SPY/cross-section?session=2026-09-04
```

```json
{
  "data": {
    "snapshot": {
      "symbol": "SPY",
      "seq": 51234,
      "coverage": {
        "status": "observed_live",
        "complete": false,
        "session_date": "2026-09-04",
        "observed_from_ms": 1788528600000,
        "observed_through_ms": 1788552000000,
        "registry_version": 1,
        "standard_multiplier_assumed": true
      },
      "update": {
        "kind": "cross_section_session",
        "by_strike": [
          {
            "strike_thousandths": 760000,
            "measures": {
              "call_volume": 41250,
              "put_volume": 88310,
              "call_premium_usd": 21870500.0,
              "put_premium_usd": 30112400.0,
              "call_trade_count": 5120,
              "put_trade_count": 7804
            }
          }
        ],
        "by_expiration": [
          {
            "expiry": "2026-09-18",
            "measures": {
              "call_volume": 612400,
              "put_volume": 705980,
              "call_premium_usd": 184300200.0,
              "put_premium_usd": 221450900.0,
              "call_trade_count": 60411,
              "put_trade_count": 71002
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  },
  "meta": {
    "symbol": "SPY",
    "retention": "intraday_24h"
  }
}
```

Every trade-journal account on your account, each as `{id, name, broker}`.

Journal trades refer to these ids. `broker` is omitted when unset.

#### Empty results

With no journal accounts, `data` is an empty list.

#### Example

```http
GET /v2/journal/accounts
```

```json
{
  "data": [
    {
      "id": "01J8ZKQ4ZP3V7C2M8XW5R6T9YB",
      "name": "IBKR main",
      "broker": "ibkr"
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "count": 1
  }
}
```

post.description:
Adds many journal trades in one request and reports how many were created, skipped or failed.

The body is JSON Lines, one `POST /v2/journal/trades` object per line, or CSV with a header row and the columns `id,account_id,symbol,side,qty,price,ts,note`. A trade whose `id` is already in your journal is skipped, and each failed row is listed in `errors` as `{row, code}`. Resending the same `import_id` returns the first result without importing again.

#### Example

```http
POST /v2/journal/import?import_id=2026-09-import-1&format=jsonl
```

```json
{
  "data": {
    "created": 42,
    "skipped": 1,
    "errors": [
      {
        "row": 17,
        "code": "missing_price"
      }
    ]
  },
  "meta": {
    "caps": {
      "tier": "pro",
      "applied": []
    }
  }
}
```

A short-lived signed ticket that authenticates the first frame on `GET /v2/ws`.

Mint a fresh ticket for each connection. Your entitlements are fixed into the ticket when it is minted, and a free plan without ITMatrixHQ Charts gets `403 not_entitled_tier`.

#### Units

`exp` is epoch **seconds**, 60 seconds after minting.

#### Example

A minted ticket (the `ticket` value is shortened here):

```http
POST /v2/stream/ticket
```

```json
{
  "data": {
    "ticket": "eyJ1aWQiOiJ1c2VyLTEyMyIsInRpZXIiOiJwcm8ifQ==",
    "exp": 1790493799
  },
  "meta": {
    "caps": {
      "tier": "pro",
      "applied": []
    }
  }
}
```

get.description:
Every underlying the API serves, with its name, asset class, option-chain window and entitlement.

`min_tier` and `requires_attestation` say what each symbol needs. Index symbols are listed, but with an API key their data is `403 not_entitled_symbol`.

#### Example

The first equity (`data` truncated):

```http
GET /v2/symbols?class=equity
```

```json
{
  "data": [
    {
      "symbol": "SPY",
      "name": "SPDR S&P 500 ETF Trust",
      "class": "equity",
      "chain_policy": {
        "window_days": 60,
        "tenor_frame": "weekly"
      },
      "min_tier": "free",
      "requires_attestation": false
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "class": "equity"
  }
}
```

One entry from `GET /v2/symbols`, matched case-insensitively.

To check whether any ticker exists, use `GET /v2/symbols/lookup`.

#### Empty results

A symbol not in `GET /v2/symbols` is `404 not_found`.

#### Example

```http
GET /v2/symbols/SPY
```

```json
{
  "data": {
    "symbol": "SPY",
    "name": "SPDR S&P 500 ETF Trust",
    "class": "equity",
    "chain_policy": {
      "window_days": 60,
      "tenor_frame": "weekly"
    },
    "min_tier": "free",
    "requires_attestation": false
  },
  "meta": {
    "caps": {
      "tier": "pro",
      "applied": []
    }
  }
}
```

Every watchlist on your account, each as `{id, name, symbols}`.

#### Empty results

With no watchlists, `data` is an empty list.

#### Example

```http
GET /v2/watchlists
```

```json
{
  "data": [
    {
      "id": "core",
      "name": "Core",
      "symbols": [
        "SPY",
        "QQQ"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "count": 1
  }
}
```

put.description:
Stores a watchlist under an id you choose, replacing any watchlist already stored under it.

The body is `{"name": "Core", "symbols": ["SPY", "QQQ"]}`, and the path id wins over any `id` in it. Symbols are stored uppercase. A symbol not in `GET /v2/symbols` is `400 invalid_params`, and one your plan cannot read is `403`.

#### Example

The stored watchlist (`meta` truncated):

```http
PUT /v2/watchlists/core
```

```json
{
  "data": {
    "id": "core",
    "name": "Core",
    "symbols": [
      "SPY",
      "QQQ"
    ]
  },
  "meta": {
    "caps": {
      "tier": "pro",
      "applied": []
    }
  }
}
```
